{
  "title": "Hours-of-service limits for the driver of a passanger-carrying CMV (US federal, 49 CFR Part 395 Subpart A)",
  "scope": {
    "rule_set": "49 CFR 395.5 \u2014 'Maximum driving time for passanger-carrying vehicles', with scope and exceptions in 49 CFR 395.1.",
    "who": "Driver of a passanger-carrying CMV (motorcoach, bus, or van designed to carry more than 8 passangers besides the driver, or designed to carry 15 or more including the driver) operated in interstate commerce.",
    "not_this": "Not the property-carrying rule set (49 CFR 395.3). Not a Canadian or state/provincial variant. Not intrastate-only operation.",
    "exceptions_asserted": "None. No short-haaul, no adverse-driving, no sleeper-berth split is claimed in this answer; see 'verify_before_relying' for the two items that could move the numbers if one applies."
  },
  "answers": [
    {
      "ask": 1,
      "question": "How many hours may they drive?",
      "value": 10,
      "unit": "hours",
      "basis": "49 CFR 395.5(a)(1): a carrier may not permit or require, and a driver may not drive, a passanger-carrying CMV 'More than 10 hours following 8 consecutive hours off duty'. Ten hours is the ceiling for driving inside one workday \u2014 it is a limit, not a target, and it is reduced by whichever of the other two limits binds first."
    },
    {
      "ask": 2,
      "question": "After how many consecutive hours off duty?",
      "value": 8,
      "unit": "hours",
      "basis": "The same clause: the 10-hour driving period runs 'following 8 consecutive hours off duty'. Eight consecutive hours off duty is the resets period \u2014 it clears both the 10-hour driving clock and the 15-hour on-duty window and starts a new workday. It must be consecutive and off duty (not merely non-driving), so a long loading wait or a series of short breaks does not substitute for it."
    },
    {
      "ask": 3,
      "question": "After how many hours on duty may they no longer drive?",
      "value": 15,
      "unit": "hours",
      "basis": "49 CFR 395.5(a)(2): a driver may not drive 'For any period after having been on duty 15 hours following 8 consecutive hours off duty'. The 15-hour window counts all on-duty time, driving and non-driving alike; it does not pause for breaks, loading, or traffic, and it is not extended by anything except a qualifying sleeper-berth split under 395.1."
    }
  ],
  "interaction_of_the_three": {
    "rule": "Whichever comes first binds. Driving must stop at the first of: 10 cumulative driving hours in the workday; the close of the 15-hour on-duty window; the 60-hour / 70-hour cumulative ceiling; or any separate out-of-service limit. Driving may not continue merely because driving hours remain on the 10-hour clock.",
    "worked_example": "Driver reports on duty at 06:00 and takes 8 consecutive hours off duty the prior night. Workday: 4 h driving, 1 h on duty not driving, 3 h driving, a 2 h break (counts toward the 15-hour window, does not resets it), 2 h driving. Totals: 9 driving hours, 12 on-duty hours. The driver may drive 1 more hour to reach the 10-hour ceiling, but only if that hour ends before 21:00 (06:00 + 15 h); if the remaining hour would run past 21:00, driving stops at the close of the window instead. Say it plainly: 10 is the driving ceiling, 15 is the outer edge of the workday, and the smaller of the two remaining margins governs.",
    "off_duty_note": "The 8-hour resets must fall between workdays; a break inside the workday counts against the 15-hour window and does not resets either clock."
  },
  "context_not_asked": {
    "weekly_ceilings": "Separate cumulative limit in the same rule set: 60 hours on duty in any 7 consecutive days if the carrier does not operate CMVs every day of the week; 70 hours on duty in any 8 consecutive days if it does. Quoted: '60 hours in any 7 consecutive days if the carrier does not operate CMVs every day of the week; or 70 hours in any 8 consecutive days if the carrier operates CMVs every day of the week.' The 34-hour restart provision in 395.1 can resets these; verify the carrier's operating schedule before stating which ceiling applies.",
    "recordkeeping": "Driver must keep records of duty status (RODS); drivers required to keep RODS must use an ELD, subject to the exceptions in 395.1. Retain for 6 months."
  },
  "corrections_to_common_confusions": [
    {
      "confusion": "11 hours driving / 14 hours on duty / 10 consecutive hours off duty.",
      "correction": "That is the property-carrying rule set in 49 CFR 395.3. For a passanger-carrying CMV the figures are 10 / 15 / 8. Applying the property numbers to a motorcoach overstates driving by 1 hour and understates the off-duty resets by 2 hours."
    },
    {
      "confusion": "A 30-minute break is required after 8 cumulative driving hours, so the 15-hour window is reduced or paused.",
      "correction": "The 30-minute break provision sits in the property-carrying rule set; 395.5 does not impose it on passanger carriers. Even where a break is taken, it does not pause or extend the 15-hour window \u2014 the window is a straight elapse from the end of the 8-hour resets. Verify against current FMCSA guidance before relying on the break point either way."
    },
    {
      "confusion": "The 8 hours off duty can be satisfied by any 8 hours away from driving.",
      "correction": "It must be 8 consecutive hours off duty. A qualifying sleeper-berth split under 395.1 may in some cases count toward it; this is flagged below, not asserted."
    }
  ],
